  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Where does the White Nile begin?",
      "a": "The White Nile flows out of Lake Victoria at Jinja in Uganda. Its farthest little streams rise even further south, in the hills of Rwanda and Burundi.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the source of the Nile?",
        "Where does the Nile start?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does the Blue Nile come from?",
      "a": "The Blue Nile flows out of Lake Tana in the highlands of Ethiopia. Heavy summer rains there make it swell and carry rich, dark mud downstream.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is it called the Blue Nile?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a confluence?",
      "a": "A confluence is the place where two rivers meet and join. The White Nile and the Blue Nile meet at Khartoum in Sudan.",
      "asks": [
        "Where do the White Nile and Blue Nile meet?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why was the Aswan High Dam built?",
      "a": "Finished in 1970, the dam controls the Nile’s floods, stores water in Lake Nasser for dry years and makes electricity. But it traps the mud that once fed the fields, so farmers now use fertiliser.",
      "asks": [
        "What does the Aswan Dam do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do most Egyptians live along the Nile?",
      "a": "Most of Egypt is desert with almost no rain. The Nile gives water for drinking and farming, so about 95 percent of Egyptians live on the narrow strip of land along the river and in the delta.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is Egypt called the gift of the Nile?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The Nile is about 6,650 km long and is often listed as the world's longest river.",
    "The White Nile and the Blue Nile meet at Khartoum, Sudan.",
    "The Aswan High Dam was completed in 1970, creating Lake Nasser."
